      "new_comments": [
        {
          "id_code": "3jbi8",
          "created_at": "2026-10-29T08:40:18Z",
          "author": "paul_irolla_6b5ae261d0224",
          "body_html": "<p>A tool list is paid on every turn it stays in the context, so the size of the list weighs more than the number of servers. The cost sits in what the model reads per request.</p>\n\n"
        },
        {
          "id_code": "3jc08",
          "created_at": "2026-10-29T10:50:25Z",
          "author": "alexar76",
          "body_html": "<p>Agreed, and your question earlier is what made us count it. Across 34,374 servers the median tool list is about 1,300 tokens, but the mean is three times that: a few servers with hundreds of tools carry most of the tokens (the 99th-percentile package is about 40,000). So which servers are connected matters more than how many. Caching shifts it again: the tools sit at the start of the prompt, so a list that gets reordered, or refetched after <code>list_changed</code>, writes the whole prefix to the cache again.</p>\n\n<p>The numbers are here: <a href=\"https://dev.to/alexar76/what-an-mcp-servers-tool-list-costs-we-counted-the-tokens-of-34374-of-them-3011\" rel=\"nofollow\">dev.to/alexar76/what-an-mcp-server...</a>. If Tokenade ever wants per-server counts, HISTOR serves them at <code>https://histor.modelmarket.dev/api/v1/servers/&lt;id&gt;/cost</code>.</p>\n\n"
        }
      ]
    },
